Here's the real tradeoff, side by side.

## The tradeoff, side by side

{% table %}
* Factor
* Cold Email
* LinkedIn Outreach
---
* Scale ceiling
* Effectively unlimited once infrastructure is built, volume scales with domains and inboxes, not headcount
* Bounded by platform connection and messaging limits per seat, scale means adding more seats, not just more sends
---
* Reply rate range we see
* 1% to 3% blended across managed campaigns, see our benchmark report for the full breakdown
* 35% on average across connection + follow-up sequences, measured against an already-warmer, connection-accepted audience
---
* Setup overhead
* Domain warm-up, SPF/DKIM/DMARC, and deliverability monitoring before you send anything close to full volume
* Account warm-up and daily connection/message caps to avoid platform restrictions, lighter technical lift, similar patience required
---
* Best-fit buyer
* Any verifiable business email, works even for buyers who aren't personally active on social platforms
* Buyers who are actually active on LinkedIn, posting, commenting, checking their inbox, which skews toward certain seniority levels and industries
---
* Personalization style
* Firmographic and intent-data personalization at scale: company size, funding, hiring signals, tech stack
* Personal and contextual: recent posts, mutual connections, profile activity, feels warmer per message but is harder to template
---
* Compliance surface
* Governed by CAN-SPAM, GDPR, and similar laws, see our compliance guide for the specifics
* Governed primarily by LinkedIn's own platform terms (connection/message limits, automation restrictions) rather than email-specific law
{% /table %}

Reply-rate range is Myntmore's own published benchmark across client engagements (see the 2026 benchmark report). Platform limits and general channel behavior reflect publicly known LinkedIn and email-provider policies, not client-specific data.

## When cold email is the better lever

You need volume. Once infrastructure is warmed up, email scales with domains and inboxes, not with a person's daily connection limit.

Your ICP includes buyers who aren't especially active on LinkedIn, common in traditional industries, manufacturing, or more senior operational roles who check email far more than their feed.

You have strong firmographic or intent-data signals (funding, hiring, tech stack) to personalize against at scale, the kind of targeting cold email is built for.

You're testing a brand-new ICP or offer and need fast, high-volume signal on what resonates before committing to a slower, relationship-led channel.

## When LinkedIn outreach is the better lever

Your buyers are genuinely active on the platform, posting, commenting, checking their inbox regularly, so a message actually gets seen.

The sale is relationship-led or complex, where seeing a prospect's recent posts and mutual connections lets you personalize in a way email data can't replicate.

You're already investing in personal branding or thought-leadership content, LinkedIn outreach compounds with that content rather than competing with it for attention.

You want the warmth of an accepted connection before the first real message lands, useful for senior, harder-to-reach titles who filter cold email aggressively.

## The honest answer: most teams shouldn't pick just one

In one of our SaaS engagements, running cold email and LinkedIn outreach in parallel, the same prospect seeing both touches within days of each other, produced a 34% reply rate and $1.2M in tracked pipeline over 8 weeks. Neither channel alone was the lever; the overlap between them was. See the full [SaaS Series A case study](/case-studies/saas-series-a) for the details, or read how we approach each channel individually on our [cold email](/services/cold-email) and [LinkedIn outreach](/services/linkedin-outreach) service pages.

If you are evaluating whether to execute outbound in-house or with an external team, check out our [DIY outbound frameworks](/services/do-it-yourself) or explore co-selling through our [agency partner program](/lp/agency-partners).
